      "material_state": "CR800",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 10,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "In contrast to the Orowan bypass mechanism, which dom - inates when precipitates are larger or incoherent, the dis - location shear mechanism prevails in this case. These L1 2 precipitates impede dislocation motion by being sheared during slip, thereby enhancing the resistance to plastic deformation. The precipitation hardening of L1 2 phase can be expressed as [63]:",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "f9534748003de852:part:003"
        },
        {
          "page": 10,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "where γAPB is the anti-phase boundary energy of L12 (∼200 mJ·m− 2) [64]. f is the volume fraction of L1 2 coherent precip- itates (∼4.0%, evaluate by TEM results). Therefore, the precipitation strengthening contribution ΔσP is calculated to be 157.4 MPa.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "f9534748003de852:part:003"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_2",
      "name": "dislocation shearing",
      "category": "deformation mechanism",
      "sample_id": "sample_2",
      "material_state": "CR800",
      "assertion_level": "directly_reported",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 10,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "In contrast to the Orowan bypass mechanism, which dom - inates when precipitates are larger or incoherent, the dis - location shear mechanism prevails in this case. These L1 2 precipitates impede dislocation motion by being sheared during slip, thereby enhancing the resistance to plastic deformation.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "f9534748003de852:part:003"
        },
        {
          "page": 10,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "The presence of SF fringes and paired dislocations (Fig. 10b) indicates that the coherent L1 2 precipitate can undergo SF shear deformation. Furthermore, the enlarged image in Fig. 10d illustrates how numerous immobile L-C locks shear and deform the L1 2 precipitates. The L12 phase in the Al1Ti2 alloy contributes to strength - ening primarily through dislocation shearing [ 62], as the precipitates are small in size and coherent with the matrix.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": "Fig. 10b, Fig. 10d",
